window.STORY_JSON="{\"inkVersion\":21,\"root\":[[\"#\",\"^CHAPTER: 1 - Something Sparkly\",\"/#\",{\"->\":\"morning\"},[\"done\",{\"#n\":\"g-0\"}],null],\"done\",{\"morning\":[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Christmas Morning\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker woke up at exactly the correct time, which is whenever Tucker wakes up.\",\"\\n\",\"^He was curled on his favorite brown leather couch, wedged between the cozy pillows like a striped loaf of bread. One golden-green eye opened. Then the other. Something was different about the house. Again.\",\"\\n\",\"^The pillows had changed. Overnight, his regular pillows had been replaced by red Christmas pillows covered in big white snowflakes, which he had never been consulted about but respected deeply. A crocheted blanket was draped over the back of the couch, and it smelled like the closet where Mom and Dad keep their winter things. Suspicious. Comfortable, but suspicious.\",\"\\n\",\"^And in the corner of the living room, where there had never been anything but a lamp and a sunbeam, there was now a TREE.\",\"\\n\",\"^A whole tree. Indoors. Green and prickly and tall, wrapped in little lights that glowed red and gold and blue. Shiny round balls hung from every branch like the world's biggest collection of cat toys. Under it sat a pile of boxes wrapped in crinkly paper and tied with ribbons.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker gave the tree his most serious stare. The tree twinkled back. Rude.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Mrrp,\\\" said Tucker, which means, roughly, \\\"I will be investigating this.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^He hopped down and did a slow lap of the living room. Socks, enormous ones, hung from the fireplace in a row, and one of them had his name stitched on it (he assumed; it had a fish on it). A little wooden village sat on the windowsill with tiny houses that glowed from inside. A bowl of something called \\\"eggnog\\\" on the kitchen counter turned out, on closer inspection, to be a drink, and not for cats.\",\"\\n\",\"^Mom and Dad were up early, humming songs about snow and hanging things on other things. Every few minutes one of them said, \\\"Tucker, don't,\\\" which Tucker understood to mean, \\\"Tucker, please continue.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^He jumped back onto the couch, nosed the new pillows until they were in the correct position, and sat with his front feet together and his tail wrapped neatly around them. This was his thinking pose. Big round eyes. White chin up. The M on his forehead furrowed in concentration. Something was going on. Something sparkly. Tucker would find out.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.morning_fun\"},{\"morning_fun\":[[\"^The morning stretched out ahead of him, full of possibilities.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Take a quick nap first. Investigations need energy.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Bat a shiny ornament off the tree.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Demand breakfast loudly.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-2\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Go see who is whispering on the fireplace mantel.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-3\",\"flg\":4},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"naps\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"naps\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker closed his eyes for \\\"just a second,\\\" which in cat time is forty-five minutes.\",\"\\n\",\"^He dreamed of a sardine wearing a tiny Santa hat. It was a good dream. When he woke up, a piece of silver tinsel was stuck to his ear, and he wore it proudly, like a festive little earring.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"mischief\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"mischief\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"has_ribbon\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^The lowest branch held a shiny red ball that showed Tucker's own face, stretched out wide and silly. Tucker sat in front of it with great dignity. He looked at Dad. He looked at the ornament. He reached out one paw...\",\"\\n\",\"^...and gently, scientifically, tapped it. Boing. It bounced off the branch, rolled across the rug (it was the unbreakable kind; Mom and Dad had learned), and disappeared under the tree.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker followed it into the green, piney darkness between the presents. And there, stuck to the side of the very biggest box, was a long curl of red ribbon dusted with tiny flecks of gold glitter.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker batted it once. Sparkle. A clue! Possibly. Everything was possibly a clue today.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-2\":[\"\\n\",\"^Tucker marched to the kitchen and announced, in a voice like a rusty trumpet, that he had NEVER been fed. Not once. Not in his entire life. Not even on holidays.\",\"\\n\",\"^Mom, who had fed him two hours ago, sighed and filled his bowl anyway. Victory tasted like turkey-flavored crunchies. Festive.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-3\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"the_nutcracker\"},null]}],null]}],\"the_nutcracker\":[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Talking Nutcracker\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker hopped onto the arm of the reading chair, and from there, with one very careful leap, onto the fireplace mantel, between a candle and a row of little glass snowmen.\",\"\\n\",\"^At the end of the mantel stood a soldier. A wooden soldier, painted red and blue and gold, with a tall black hat, a fluffy white beard, and a big square jaw full of painted teeth.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker stared at it. It stared back, very straight and very still.\",\"\\n\",\"^Then it spoke.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"HALT! Who goes there?\\\" boomed the wooden soldier, his jaw clacking up and down. \\\"Oh! It's YOU. The famous Tucker! The stripiest detective on the whole street! Barnaby the bat told me all about you before they packed him away for the winter!\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker's ears swiveled forward. So he was famous. He had suspected as much.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"I am Sergeant Walnut,\\\" said the soldier, \\\"Official Guard of Christmas. I stand on this mantel and I watch EVERYTHING. And I have terrible news.\\\" He lowered his voice to a wooden whisper. \\\"The Christmas Star is missing.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker's tail went straight up like a question mark.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"The golden star that goes on the very top of the tree!\\\" said Sergeant Walnut. \\\"Every Christmas Eve it shines from the top so the whole house glows. But this morning, when Mom and Dad opened the decoration box... it was GONE. The tree has nothing on top! Look at it! It's practically wearing no hat!\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker looked. The tree did look a little bit bald.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"I can't go look myself,\\\" said the Sergeant. \\\"I'm a nutcracker. My legs do not bend. But you... you have paws. And whiskers. And a mysterious letter M on your forehead, which surely stands for... Merry.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Mrrrow,\\\" said Tucker. (It stood for \\\"Me.\\\" Everyone knew that.)\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Find three clues before the carolers come tonight,\\\" said Sergeant Walnut, \\\"and you'll find the star. Check the wrapping-paper pile in the hallway. Or ask the snowman out front. He's frosty, but he notices things. And, um... someone has been sniffling in the hall closet, inside the box of Halloween decorations. A little someone. A little someone who glows. He might know something.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"has_ribbon\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"\\n\",\"^Tucker dropped the curl of red ribbon at the Sergeant's painted boots.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Ah-HA!\\\" Walnut clacked. \\\"Red ribbon with gold glitter, from under the tree! That's clue number one, detective. Things that wear bows... remember that.\\\"\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.32\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"^The clock ticked toward noon. The afternoon was waiting.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker sat back on his haunches and considered the nutcracker. Was a talking wooden soldier trustworthy? Probably not. But it was the most interesting thing that had happened since Halloween, and Tucker was not about to let a mystery go to waste.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"One more thing,\\\" Sergeant Walnut added. \\\"Whoever took the star was sneaky. Very sneaky. Sneakier than a cat. I know that sounds impossible.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker's tail lashed once. Nobody was sneakier than a cat. That was practically the law. He had proved it before, and he would prove it again.\",\"\\n\",\"^He touched his nose to the Sergeant's painted nose to seal the deal. It smelled like old wood and peppermint. \\\"At ease, detective,\\\" Walnut said, and his jaw clacked into what might have been a smile. \\\"Now go!\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Out in the hallway, the afternoon light was already stretching long and golden across the floor. Three trails. Three suspects. One very important star.\",\"\\n\",\"^Where should a serious detective begin? The wrapping-paper pile. The frosty snowman. The little someone who glows, sniffling in the closet.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker flicked his tail. He would decide after a short stretch. A very short one. Probably.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"ch1_done\",\"re\":true},\"^TO BE CONTINUED in Chapter 2: Three Clues... 🐾\",\"\\n\",\"#\",\"^CHAPTER_END: 1\",\"/#\",{\"->\":\"chapter2_start\"},\"#\",\"^CHAPTER: 2 - Three Clues\",\"/#\",null],\"chapter2_start\":[[\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"ch1_done\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"afternoon\"},{\"->\":\".^.^.^.4\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Talking Nutcracker\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^PREVIOUSLY... Tucker woke up to a house gone twinkly overnight, and Sergeant Walnut, a talking nutcracker on the mantel, gave him a mission: the Christmas Star is missing from the top of the tree! Find three clues before the carolers come tonight. Check the wrapping-paper pile, ask the frosty snowman out front, or find the little someone sniffling in the hall closet.\",\"\\n\",\"^Before he'd even left the living room, had Tucker already turned up a clue?\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Yes! A curl of red ribbon with gold glitter, stuck to the biggest present under the tree.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Not yet. He was busy demanding breakfast.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"has_ribbon\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"mischief\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"mischief\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Clue number one: things that wear bows. Sergeant Walnut had said to remember that.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",\"^A detective must keep his strength up. The clues weren't going anywhere. Probably.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"g-0\":[\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"ch1_done\",\"re\":true},{\"->\":\"afternoon\"},null]}],null],\"afternoon\":[[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Twinkly Hallway\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^The afternoon sun came in long and golden, and the hallway was wrapped in a fat green garland that blinked with tiny lights. Tucker walked along the stripes of sunlight like a tightrope, very professionally, batting at the garland only twice.\",\"\\n\",\"^At one end of the hall, the wrapping table was buried in paper and ribbons. Outside the front window, a snowman stood in the yard with his stick arms out. And the hall closet door was open just a crack... and from inside came a very small sniffle.\",\"\\n\",\"^Where should a serious detective begin?\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Dive into the wrapping-paper pile.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Go outside and stalk the snowman.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Peek into the hall closet.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-2\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"wrapping_pile\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"snowman\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-2\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"pip\"},{\"#f\":5}]}],null],\"wrapping_pile\":[[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Wrapping Paper Fort\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"searched_wrapping\",\"re\":true},\"^The wrapping table had overflowed onto the floor, and the floor had become a mountain. Crinkly paper with snowflakes. Crinkly paper with reindeer. Shiny paper, plaid paper, paper with tiny penguins in hats. Spools of ribbon. Tubes. Boxes. Tape.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker did what all cats do when they see a pile of crinkly paper. He did not decide to jump in. His body simply decided for him.\",\"\\n\",\"^Crrrrinkle-FLOOMP.\",\"\\n\",\"^Inside, it was a whole world. A paper cave. A ribbon jungle. An empty box that was exactly, perfectly, scientifically Tucker-sized.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Tunnel through to the very bottom.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Wrestle a spool of curly ribbon.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Curl up in the empty box and take a nap.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-2\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",\"^Down, down, past a roll of snowman paper and a lost pair of scissors (Tucker stayed far away from those), his paw touched something sparkly and gritty.\",\"\\n\",\"^Glitter. GOLD glitter. Star-colored glitter. A whole little trail of it, leading out of the pile and across the hallway rug... and toward the presents under the tree.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"has_glitter\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker sneezed. Now HE was also glittery. That was fine. Detectives should sparkle at Christmas.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"mischief\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"mischief\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker grabbed the end of a curly red ribbon and kicked it with both back feet, the way you defeat an enemy ribbon. It unspooled across the whole hallway. He was now wearing most of it.\",\"\\n\",\"^When he finally won, he spotted it: a trail of gold glitter sneaking out from under the pile and heading toward the presents under the tree.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"has_glitter\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-2\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"naps\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"naps\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^The box was the perfect size. Tucker made three circles and folded himself in like a present that wraps itself.\",\"\\n\",\"^He slept until a sunbeam poked him in the eye. As he stretched, he noticed a thin trail of gold glitter on the floor, leading toward the presents under the tree, though by now half of it was stuck to his own belly.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"has_glitter\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"g-0\":[\"^Tucker climbed out of the paper mountain wearing a sticky bow on his back that he did not notice for the rest of the afternoon.\",\"\\n\",\"^Mom and Dad, he noticed, had been wrapping in a big hurry. Some of the presents were wrapped very neatly. Some of them looked like they had been wrapped by a raccoon in the dark.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"afternoon_snack\"},null]}],null],\"snowman\":[[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Snowman Yard\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"met_snowman\",\"re\":true},\"^Tucker slipped out the cat flap and into the front yard, and immediately regretted it, because the front yard was made of snow.\",\"\\n\",\"^Cold snow. Wet snow. Snow that got between his toes. He lifted each paw high and shook it, like he was walking on a stove.\",\"\\n\",\"^There, in the middle of the yard, stood the snowman. He had a carrot nose, two coal-black eyes, a red-and-green knitted scarf, and two stick arms that looked extremely fun to chew.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker dropped into his stalking position: belly low (cold), tail twitching, bottom wiggling. He crept forward one paw at a time.\",\"\\n\",\"^Crunch. Crunch. Crunch.\",\"\\n\",\"^(Snow is the worst for sneaking. Everybody knows this. Tucker did it anyway.)\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Pounce on his stick arm!\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Sit very still and stare at him until he gets uncomfortable.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Politely rub against his snowy side and purr.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-2\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"mischief\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"mischief\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker launched himself and caught the end of a stick arm in his teeth. He pulled. The stick pulled back.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"OW,\\\" said the snowman. \\\"Do you MIND? That's my good arm.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker let go and sat down instantly, in the snow, pretending he had never done anything in his life. His bottom got very cold. He kept pretending.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",\"^Tucker sat down in the snow and stared. And stared. And stared. Not blinking. His golden-green eyes became two full moons of judgment.\",\"\\n\",\"^After four whole minutes, the snowman cracked. (A little bit. Down one side.)\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Okay! OKAY! What do you WANT, cat?\\\"\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-2\":[\"\\n\",\"^Tucker strolled up and rubbed his cheek against the snowman's round tummy, purring like a tiny motorboat. It was freezing. He did it anyway, for justice.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Oh,\\\" said the snowman, surprised. \\\"Well. That's... that's very warm of you, actually. Nobody hugs snowmen. Nobody's that brave.\\\"\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"g-0\":[\"^\\\"I'm Frostwick,\\\" said the snowman, straightening his scarf with one stick hand. \\\"I watch this yard day and night. Nothing gets past me. Well, the mail carrier gets past me. But nothing ELSE.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker blinked slowly, which is how cats say \\\"Go on.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"You're looking for the Christmas Star, aren't you? I could see the bald tree through the window.\\\" Frostwick leaned closer, and his voice went all crunchy and secret. \\\"I'll give you a riddle. It's the best I can do. I'm made of snow; I'm not a detective.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^He cleared his throat. \\\"It wears a bow but has no hair. It's dressed in paper, sitting there. Under the branches, big and square... the thing you seek is hiding THERE.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"heard_riddle\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker thought about this very hard. He thought about bows. He thought about hair. He thought about dinner. Then he thought about bows again.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Also,\\\" said Frostwick, \\\"you've got snow on your nose. Just so you know.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^He did. Tucker went back inside with as much dignity as a cat can have while wearing a snow mustache.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"afternoon_snack\"},null]}],null],\"pip\":[[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Pip Closet\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"met_pip\",\"re\":true},\"^The hall closet was dark and cozy and smelled like winter coats. On the floor sat a big plastic bin with its lid pushed open, full of Halloween decorations packed away for the year: a pumpkin bucket, a string of orange lights, and Barnaby the paper bat, sound asleep and snoring very softly.\",\"\\n\",\"^And on top of the bin, wrapped up in a long tangle of silver tinsel, sat a small white sheet. It was sniffling.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"T-Tucker?\\\" said the sheet. \\\"Is that you?\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^It was Pip. The little ghost from Halloween, about the size of a pillowcase, with two round black eyes and a wobbly little mouth. He was floating an inch above the bin and dripping very small, very sad tears that turned into tiny snowflakes before they hit anything.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"I came back to visit,\\\" Pip sniffled. \\\"I heard Christmas was the happiest night of the year. But I've never had a Christmas before, and I don't know how to do it. Everybody gives everybody presents. But I can't hold anything for very long. Things just fall right through me.\\\" He held up a corner of tinsel. It slid off his sheet and drifted to the floor. \\\"See? I wanted to give your Mom and Dad a present for letting me stay in their closet. But I have NOTHING to give.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Pip's sheet drooped.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Help Pip find a present he can give.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Pounce on the tinsel. It's SPARKLY and it's RIGHT THERE.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"pip.pip_help\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"pip.pip_pounce\"},{\"#f\":5}]}],{\"pip_help\":[\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"kind_to_pip\",\"re\":true},\"^Tucker hopped up onto the bin and sat next to Pip. He did not say anything. He just leaned against the little ghost, gently, the way he leans on Mom and Dad when they are sad.\",\"\\n\",\"^Pip was cold, like a breeze from an open freezer. Tucker did not mind. It was Christmas, after all. Cold was practically the theme.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"You'll help me?\\\" Pip whispered. \\\"Really?\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker blinked slowly. That was a yes.\",\"\\n\",\"^Together they thought. Tucker looked in a coat pocket (a mitten, a cough drop, no ideas). He looked inside a boot (dark, smelly, no ideas). He looked at Pip for a long time.\",\"\\n\",\"^Then Tucker had an idea, the kind of idea only a cat would have. He hopped onto the little closet window and breathed on the cold glass, the way he does when he is watching birds. Huff. A foggy cloud appeared.\",\"\\n\",\"^Then he looked at Pip.\",\"\\n\",\"^Pip floated up beside him and, very carefully, touched one corner of his sheet to the glass.\",\"\\n\",\"^Crackle-crackle-crackle. Out from Pip's touch grew frost. Lacy, silvery frost, curling into ferns and feathers and tiny perfect snowflakes, spreading across the whole window like a picture made of starlight.\",\"\\n\",\"^Pip gasped. \\\"Did I do that? I did that! Ghosts are COLD, Tucker! I can make frost! I can draw on every window in the house! That's a present nobody else in the world can give!\\\" Pip spun in the air like a happy pillowcase. \\\"You FOUND it, Tucker!\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Pip hugged Tucker's face. It was cold and a little bit like being sneezed on by a snowflake. Tucker allowed it.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"I owe you, detective,\\\" Pip said seriously. \\\"And I'll tell you a secret. Last night, very late, I saw your Mom wrapping presents in a big hurry, yawning and yawning. Something shiny and pointy went into the biggest box with the biggest bow. And Tucker... if you ever need me tonight, just say my name.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"pip_hint\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",{\"->\":\"afternoon_snack\"},null],\"pip_pounce\":[\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"mischief\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"mischief\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker's bottom wiggled. His eyes went huge and black. The tinsel was shiny. The tinsel was MOVING. Every instinct in his striped body shouted the same word: TINSEL.\",\"\\n\",\"^He pounced.\",\"\\n\",\"^He went straight through Pip and landed in a pile of winter scarves, which, to be fair, was also wonderful.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Hey!\\\" said Pip, now mostly behind a vacuum cleaner. \\\"That's exactly what everybody does!\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker poked his head out of the scarves. He did feel a tiny bit bad. Only a tiny bit. The scarves were very soft.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Fine,\\\" Pip huffed. \\\"I'll tell you one thing, just so you leave. Last night I saw your Mom wrapping presents in a big hurry. Something shiny and pointy went into the biggest box. Now shoo.\\\" He paused, and his little mouth wobbled. \\\"Merry Christmas, I guess. Whatever that means.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Pip vanished with a sad little fwip!\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"pip_hint\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker stayed in the scarves for twenty more minutes, thinking about what he had done. Mostly he thought about the scarves.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"afternoon_snack\"},null]}],\"afternoon_snack\":[[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Cookie Kitchen\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^By late afternoon the house smelled like gingerbread and Mom and Dad were rushing around, setting out a plate of cookies and a glass of milk for Santa by the fireplace. Tucker sat on the kitchen counter, where he was absolutely not allowed, beside a tray of fresh sugar cookies shaped like stars and trees, and reviewed his case.\",\"\\n\",\"^Clues found: \",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},\"out\",\"/ev\",\"^. \",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},2,\">=\",\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ Tucker's whiskers tingled. He was close. He could feel it.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.17\"},null]}],[{\"->\":\".^.b\"},{\"b\":[\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"==\",\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ One clue. A good start. Not quite enough to crack the case, but a detective never gives up, unless there is a warm spot by the fire.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.7\"},null]}],[{\"->\":\".^.b\"},{\"b\":[\"^No clues yet. Hmm.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.7\"},null]}],\"nop\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.17\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"has_ribbon\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ He remembered the curl of red ribbon with gold glitter on the biggest present.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.23\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"has_glitter\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ He remembered the trail of gold glitter leading toward the presents under the tree.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.29\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"heard_riddle\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ He remembered Frostwick's riddle: it wears a bow but has no hair, dressed in paper, under the branches, big and square.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.35\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"pip_hint\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ He remembered Pip saying something shiny and pointy went into the biggest box.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.41\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Knock a cookie cutter off the counter, for luck.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Steal a lick of frosting.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Ignore all snacks. Think detective thoughts.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-2\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"mischief\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"mischief\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Ting! The star-shaped cookie cutter hit the floor. Mom said \\\"TUCKER.\\\" Tucker looked at her as if he had never seen a cookie cutter before in his life. Luck acquired.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",\"^Tucker leaned toward the bowl of white frosting. Mom whisked it away just in time. Tucker licked the counter where it had been instead. It tasted like counter. Disappointing but educational.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-2\":[\"\\n\",\"^Tucker closed his eyes and thought very hard. The thoughts went: star... bow... box... star... nap... no, star... It was a lot of thinking for one cat.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"g-0\":[\"^Outside, the sky was going pink and purple and then deep, soft blue. Snow began to fall in big, slow flakes. Up and down the street, the houses lit up one by one with colored lights.\",\"\\n\",\"^Christmas Eve was almost here.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker hopped down and patrolled the house one last time while the light faded. The tree glowed in the corner, still bald on top. The stockings hung in their row. On the mantel, Sergeant Walnut gave him a stiff little wooden salute.\",\"\\n\",\"^In the hallway mirror, Tucker caught sight of himself: brown and gray stripes, dark rings around his tail, white chin, big golden-green eyes. Handsome, obviously. But was he the face of a cat who would solve the greatest mystery of Christmas? He narrowed his eyes. He tilted his head. He decided yes.\",\"\\n\",\"^Then he ate a few more crunchies, because detectives must keep their strength up, and went to sit by the tree.\",\"\\n\",\"^Then the hallway light clicked on, and Dad's voice called from the bedroom: \\\"Tuuucker! Come here, buddy... we got you something to WEAR for Christmas.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker's ears went flat. Wear? AGAIN?\",\"\\n\",\"^Somewhere behind him, the tree lights twinkled. The carolers would be here soon. Christmas Eve was coming, ready or not.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"ch2_done\",\"re\":true},\"^TO BE CONTINUED in Chapter 3: Christmas Eve... 🎄\",\"\\n\",\"#\",\"^CHAPTER_END: 2\",\"/#\",{\"->\":\"chapter3_start\"},\"#\",\"^CHAPTER: 3 - Christmas Eve\",\"/#\",null]}],null],\"chapter3_start\":[[\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"ch2_done\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"evening\"},{\"->\":\".^.^.^.4\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Cookie Kitchen\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^PREVIOUSLY... The Christmas Star has vanished from the top of the tree, and Tucker, the stripiest detective on the street, has spent all of Christmas Eve hunting for clues. Now the snow is falling, the cookies are out for Santa, and the carolers are on their way.\",\"\\n\",\"^Had Tucker found the curl of red ribbon on the biggest present that morning?\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Yes, after \\\"accidentally\\\" batting an ornament under the tree.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^No, he was busy with breakfast.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"has_ribbon\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"mischief\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"mischief\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"g-0\":[\"^And which trail did he follow that afternoon?\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^The wrapping-paper pile. He found a trail of gold glitter leading to the presents under the tree.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-2\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^The snowman. Frostwick gave him a riddle: \\\"It wears a bow but has no hair, dressed in paper, under the branches, big and square.\\\"\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-3\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Little ghost Pip. Tucker helped him discover he can draw frost on windows, and Pip promised to help if Tucker ever needed him.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-4\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Little ghost Pip... but Tucker pounced on the tinsel. Oops. Pip still told him something shiny went into the biggest box.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-5\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-2\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"searched_wrapping\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"has_glitter\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker was still a little bit glittery. He considered it a look.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.g-1\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-3\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"met_snowman\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"heard_riddle\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker's toes were still a little bit cold. He considered it a sacrifice.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.g-1\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-4\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"met_pip\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"kind_to_pip\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"pip_hint\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Somewhere in the house, a window crackled softly as a new frost fern grew across it. Tucker felt warm all the way to his whiskers.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.g-1\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-5\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"met_pip\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"pip_hint\",\"re\":true},\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},1,\"+\",{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\",\"re\":true},\"/ev\",\"^Tucker felt a tiny bit bad about that. Only a tiny bit.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.g-1\"},{\"#f\":5}]}],\"g-1\":[\"^Clues found: \",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},\"out\",\"/ev\",\"^. \",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},2,\">=\",\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ Tucker's whiskers tingled. He was close.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.13\"},null]}],[{\"->\":\".^.b\"},{\"b\":[\"^One clue. Not quite enough to crack the case. But the night was young.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.13\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",true,\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"ch2_done\",\"re\":true},{\"->\":\"evening\"},null]}],null],\"evening\":[[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Sweater Mirror\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^Mom and Dad came into the hallway together. Dad, with his gray beard and glasses, was holding something red and green and woolly. Mom, with her dark curly hair, was holding something with antlers. And they were both smiling the smile that means something is about to happen to you.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Tucker,\\\" said Mom, \\\"it's Christmas Eve. Everybody dresses up a little.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker looked at the sweater. He looked at the antlers. He looked at the door. He considered running under the bed. But a detective must sometimes go undercover. Even in wool.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"searched_wrapping\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ He recognized the sweater from the wrapping pile. It had clearly been waiting for him.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.12\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"^So, what should Tucker wear?\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^The Christmas sweater: red and green, with a snowflake on the front.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^The reindeer antlers: fuzzy, brown, with tiny bells.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Just a jingle bell. That's enough Christmas for one cat.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-2\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^sweater\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"outfit\",\"re\":true},\"^The sweater went on over his head. Tucker immediately fell over sideways and lay perfectly still, like a striped loaf of bread that had given up. \\\"He's fine,\\\" said Dad. \\\"He's being dramatic.\\\" Tucker was being dramatic. After one long, slow minute, Mom sighed and took the sweater off again.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^antlers\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"outfit\",\"re\":true},\"^The antlers went on between his ears. Tucker sat very still. Then he shook his head once, hard. The antlers flew across the hallway and landed on the vacuum cleaner, which, honestly, looked better in them.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-2\":[\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^bell\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"VAR=\":\"outfit\",\"re\":true},\"^Tucker sat down and looked at Mom and Dad with his most reasonable expression. Dad laughed. \\\"Okay, buddy. Fair enough.\\\"\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.g-0\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"g-0\":[\"^In the end, Mom clipped a little red collar around his neck, with one shiny gold jingle bell hanging right under his white chin.\",\"\\n\",\"^Jingle.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker looked in the mirror. A handsome, festive detective stared back. The bell was, he had to admit, quite distinguished. And it would make sneaking completely impossible. He decided that was the bell's problem.\",\"\\n\",\"^Then, from outside, came a sound. Singing. Real singing, in the snow, right on the front step.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"carolers\"},null]}],null],\"carolers\":[[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Carolers at the Door\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^The front door swung open, and there they were: the carolers. A grandpa with a lantern. A girl in a puffy purple coat. Two little brothers in matching striped hats. A teenager holding a songbook and a thermos of cocoa. Snow was falling all around them in big, soft flakes, and they were singing about silent nights and jingle bells.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"KITTY!\\\" said the little brothers, which was not in the song.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker walked out to the doormat, jingling with every step.\",\"\\n\",\"^The cold air smelled like snow and woodsmoke and peppermint. Up and down the street, every house glowed with lights: red ones, green ones, blue ones, and one house with so many white lights it looked like a birthday cake. Behind him, the tree in the window twinkled, still bald on top, waiting.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker had never heard so many small humans singing at once. They were loud. They were a little off-key. They were, he decided, acceptable.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"met_snowman\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ Out in the yard, Frostwick the snowman was humming along. Badly. Snowmen have no ear for music.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.16\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},2,\">=\",\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ Then Tucker's tail went straight up. A bow, but no hair. Dressed in paper. Under the branches. Big and square. Gold glitter. Something shiny and pointy. All the clues snapped together in his brain like a puzzle made of fish.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.25\"},null]}],[{\"->\":\".^.b\"},{\"b\":[\"^Tucker looked around. There was so much going on. The case was still a muddle in his head, but the night was big, and full of choices.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.25\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"^From the mantel inside, Sergeant Walnut clacked his jaw. \\\"This is it, detective!\\\" he boomed. \\\"Where is the Christmas Star?\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Investigate the biggest present with the big red bow.\",\"/str\",{\"VAR?\":\"clues_found\"},2,\">=\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":5},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Climb the tree to look for the star from the very top.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Forget the case. Go sing with the carolers.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-2\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Curl up in an empty gift box. Just to... think.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-3\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"big_present\"},null],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"ending_tree_topper\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-2\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"ending_caroling\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-3\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"ending_gift_box\"},{\"#f\":5}]}],null],\"big_present\":[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Big Present\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker trotted back inside, jingling, and marched straight under the tree. The carolers followed in a hushed little line, still holding their songbooks.\",\"\\n\",\"^There it was. The biggest present of all, wrapped in shiny red paper, with an enormous red bow on top. A bow, but no hair. Dressed in paper. Under the branches. Big and square. Sprinkled all over with tiny flecks of gold glitter.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker hooked the end of the ribbon with one claw. He pulled.\",\"\\n\",\"^The bow came undone with a long, satisfying zzzzip.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Tucker!\\\" said Mom. \\\"That's not for... oh. Oh no.\\\" She came closer and looked at the box. \\\"Wait. Did I wrap this one? I don't remember wrapping this one. I was SO tired last night.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Dad knelt down, and together they peeled back the paper. Inside the box was a nest of tissue paper. And inside the tissue paper, glowing gold in the tree lights, was a star.\",\"\\n\",\"^THE Christmas Star.\",\"\\n\",\"^The carolers gasped. Sergeant Walnut cheered so hard his jaw stuck open.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"I wrapped the STAR,\\\" said Mom, laughing so hard she had to sit down on the rug. \\\"I must have scooped it up with the wrapping paper. Tucker, you found it!\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^(So it was Mom all along. Tucker had suspected as much.)\",\"\\n\",\"^Under the star, at the bottom of the box, was one more thing: a little star-shaped toy that smelled like catnip, with a fish drawn on the tag. Tucker sniffed it. It smelled like victory.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"kind_to_pip\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"\\n\",\"^Then, from somewhere near the window, came a small, brave crackling sound, and a lacy frost star bloomed across the glass.\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.pip_returns\"},{\"->\":\".^.^.^.28\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"ending_star\"},{\"pip_returns\":[[\"^It was Pip, peeking out from behind the curtain, glowing faintly against the snowy dark.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"You found it, Tucker!\\\" Pip whispered. \\\"I knew you would. The ghosts are having a Snow Parade tonight, all around the neighborhood, drawing frost on every window for Christmas... and, um... they said I could bring a friend. If I had one.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker looked at the star. He looked at Mom and Dad, laughing on the rug. He looked at the little ghost, who was twisting the corners of his sheet hopefully.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Give Pip the star-shaped toy as his first Christmas present, and join the Snow Parade.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-0\",\"flg\":20},\"ev\",\"str\",\"^Thank Pip, but stay to help put the star on top of the tree.\",\"/str\",\"/ev\",{\"*\":\".^.c-1\",\"flg\":20},{\"c-0\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"ending_snow_parade\"},{\"#f\":5}],\"c-1\":[\"\\n\",{\"->\":\"ending_star\"},{\"#f\":5}]}],null]}],\"ending_star\":[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Ending Christmas Star\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^Dad picked Tucker up and set him on his shoulder, the way he does on very special occasions. Mom handed Tucker the star. Well. She held it, and Tucker put one paw on it, which counts.\",\"\\n\",\"^Together they reached up, up, up, to the very top of the tree.\",\"\\n\",\"^Click.\",\"\\n\",\"^The star slid into place, and the whole tree seemed to take a breath and glow brighter. Gold light spilled across the ceiling and the presents and the snowy window, where the carolers pressed their mittens to the glass and cheered.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Tucker,\\\" said Mom, \\\"you are officially the best detective in the whole world.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker agreed. Modestly.\",\"\\n\",\"^The carolers sang one more song, just for him, and the little brothers called it \\\"The Ballad of Tucker the Star Finder.\\\" It had only one line, and the line was \\\"TUCKER!\\\", but it was a very good song.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"met_snowman\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ Out in the yard, Frostwick the snowman tipped his stick hand. \\\"Told you,\\\" he crunched. \\\"Big and square.\\\"\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.20\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"mischief\"},3,\">=\",\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ (On his way down from Dad's shoulder, Tucker knocked one more ornament off the tree. A hero has duties.)\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.28\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"^On the mantel, Sergeant Walnut called it the greatest detective work in Christmas history. Later that night, Tucker fell asleep under the glowing tree, curled around his new star toy, while the snow fell and fell outside.\",\"\\n\",\"^THE END — Tucker, the Star of Christmas. ⭐\",\"\\n\",\"#\",\"^ENDING: christmas_star\",\"/#\",\"end\",null],\"ending_snow_parade\":[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Ending Snow Parade\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^Dad lifted the Christmas Star onto the top of the tree, and the whole house glowed gold. Then Tucker picked up the little star-shaped toy in his mouth, padded over to the window, and set it down in front of Pip.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"For... me?\\\" Pip whispered. \\\"A present? My very first Christmas present?\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Pip could not technically hold it for very long, because he was a ghost. But he wrapped his sheet around it and hugged it and said \\\"thank you\\\" eleven times, which is what matters.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Come on!\\\" Pip cheered. \\\"It's PARADE time!\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Out of the snowy dark floated the ghosts. Dozens of them. Big ghosts and little ghosts, a ghost in a tiny knitted hat, a ghost wearing a bow, an old ghost with spectacles who said \\\"Merry Christmas, young sir\\\" to Tucker. They glowed soft and silvery in the falling snow, and they weren't scary at all. They looked like a parade of floating clean laundry, which, to a cat, is the most beautiful thing in the world.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker hopped through the cat flap and took his place at the front of the line, his gold bell jingling, his paws leaving a neat trail of prints in the fresh snow.\",\"\\n\",\"^Around the block they went: past the houses, past the twinkling lights, past Frostwick the snowman, who waved one stick arm and called, \\\"Now THAT'S a Christmas!\\\" And at every single window, the ghosts touched the cold glass, and lacy frost grew across it in the shapes of stars and ferns and snowflakes. A present for every house on the street, from the littlest ghost.\",\"\\n\",\"^The carolers followed behind, singing, and some of them (the brave ones) walked right in the middle of the ghosts.\",\"\\n\",\"^At the very end, back on the front step, Pip floated up and touched his tiny cold forehead to the M on Tucker's.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"Thank you for my first Christmas,\\\" he said. \\\"Best friends?\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker blinked slowly. Very slowly. The slowest blink of his whole life.\",\"\\n\",\"^Later that night, back on his brown leather couch, between the red Christmas pillows, Tucker fell asleep with a full tummy and snow still melting on his whiskers. And if you'd looked closely, you'd have seen a little white ghost curled up on the pillow beside him, hugging a star-shaped toy, snoring softly. \\\"Merry,\\\" Pip mumbled in his sleep. \\\"Merry.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^THE END — The Secret Snow Parade. 👻❄️\",\"\\n\",\"#\",\"^ENDING: secret_snow_parade\",\"/#\",\"end\",null],\"ending_tree_topper\":[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Ending Tree Topper\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^If the star could not be found, Tucker decided, then the tree would simply need a new star. And the most important, most handsome, most golden-eyed thing in the house was obviously him.\",\"\\n\",\"^He crouched. He wiggled. He leaped onto the lowest branch.\",\"\\n\",\"^Jingle. Rustle. Climb. Jingle. Rustle. CLIMB.\",\"\\n\",\"^Ornaments swung. Lights blinked. Tinsel fluttered down like silver rain. The whole tree leaned a little to the left, then a little to the right, while Mom shouted \\\"TUCKER!\\\" and Dad dove to hold the trunk.\",\"\\n\",\"^And then Tucker was at the top. He sat on the very highest branch, perfectly balanced, his white chin up, his tail curled around the trunk, his gold bell shining in the lights.\",\"\\n\",\"^The carolers at the window gasped. Then they cheered. \\\"The tree has a CAT on it!\\\" yelled one of the little brothers. \\\"Best. Christmas. EVER.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^Dad, still holding the trunk, started laughing. Mom took about forty photos. In every single one, Tucker looked like a king.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"mischief\"},3,\">=\",\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ (A few ornaments did not survive the climb. Tucker felt they had been given a good life.)\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.22\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"^Much later, when Tucker had been carefully lifted down, they found the real star. It was wrapped up inside the biggest present with the big red bow. Mom had wrapped it by mistake, half asleep. (So it was Mom all along. Tucker had suspected as much.)\",\"\\n\",\"^But the next year, and the year after that, the family's Christmas card always had the same picture on the front: a brown tabby cat with golden-green eyes, sitting proudly on top of the tree.\",\"\\n\",\"^THE END — Tucker, Tree Topper. 🎄🐈\",\"\\n\",\"#\",\"^ENDING: tree_topper\",\"/#\",\"end\",null],\"ending_caroling\":[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Ending Caroling Cat\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^Stars could wait. There was singing to do.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker hopped onto the front step, right in the middle of the carolers, sat up very straight, opened his mouth, and joined in.\",\"\\n\",\"^\\\"MRRROOOOW-OW-OWWW.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"^The carolers stopped. They looked at each other. Then the grandpa with the lantern laughed so hard his beard shook, and he said, \\\"Well, he's got the spirit!\\\" and they all started singing again, louder this time, with Tucker yowling along on every chorus.\",\"\\n\",\"^They sang at the house next door. They sang at the house with all the white lights. They sang at the house across the street, where an old lady in a bathrobe came out and gave everyone cookies, and gave Tucker a little piece of turkey on a napkin. Tucker decided caroling was the greatest invention in human history.\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"met_snowman\"},\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ Frostwick the snowman hummed along from the yard. For once, Tucker was even more off-key than he was.\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.16\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"naps\"},2,\">=\",\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ (Halfway through \\\"Deck the Halls,\\\" Tucker took a very short nap on the grandpa's boot. Nobody minded.)\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.24\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"^When they finally brought him home, his whiskers were full of snowflakes and his bell had jingled so much it was practically tired. Dad wrapped him in the crocheted blanket. Mom found the real star the next morning, wrapped up by mistake inside the biggest present. (So it was Mom all along. Tucker had suspected as much.)\",\"\\n\",\"^But nobody on the street ever forgot the Christmas Eve that a striped cat in a jingle bell sang with the carolers. Every year after that, they stopped at his house first.\",\"\\n\",\"^THE END — The Caroling Cat. 🎶\",\"\\n\",\"#\",\"^ENDING: caroling_cat\",\"/#\",\"end\",null],\"ending_gift_box\":[\"^@SCENE_UPDATE: background=\\\"Ending Gift Box Nap\\\", transition=crossfade, duration=1\",\"\\n\",\"^Under the tree, next to the presents, sat an empty gift box with its lid off. It was exactly, perfectly, scientifically Tucker-sized.\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker did not decide to get in. His body simply decided for him.\",\"\\n\",\"^Floomp.\",\"\\n\",\"^It was warm. It was cozy. It had a little bit of tissue paper at the bottom, which crinkled in the most relaxing way. The tree lights glowed red and gold and blue above him. The carolers sang softly outside. Tucker closed his eyes, just to think about the case for a second.\",\"\\n\",\"^He was asleep before the second verse.\",\"\\n\",\"^Mom found him there a little later. She smiled, and very quietly, she draped a long curl of red ribbon over the box and tied it into a bow, right over his head. \\\"There,\\\" she whispered. \\\"The best present under the tree.\\\"\",\"\\n\",\"ev\",{\"VAR?\":\"naps\"},1,\">=\",\"/ev\",[{\"->\":\".^.b\",\"c\":true},{\"b\":[\"^ (It was not his first nap of the day. Not even close. A personal Christmas record.)\",{\"->\":\".^.^.^.20\"},null]}],\"nop\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker slept through the whole night, through the snow, through the quiet, through a tiny jingle of sleigh bells on the roof that may or may not have been real. And when he woke up on Christmas morning, there was a new star-shaped catnip toy in his stocking, and Mom was laughing, holding up the real Christmas Star. She had wrapped it inside the biggest present by mistake. (So it was Mom all along. Tucker had suspected as much.)\",\"\\n\",\"^Tucker stretched, yawned, and decided he had solved the case. In his sleep. Which is the most advanced kind of detective work there is.\",\"\\n\",\"^THE END — The Best Present Under the Tree. 🎁\",\"\\n\",\"#\",\"^ENDING: gift_box_nap\",\"/#\",\"end\",null],\"global decl\":[\"ev\",false,{\"VAR=\":\"kind_to_pip\"},false,{\"VAR=\":\"met_pip\"},false,{\"VAR=\":\"met_snowman\"},false,{\"VAR=\":\"searched_wrapping\"},0,{\"VAR=\":\"clues_found\"},\"str\",\"^\",\"/str\",{\"VAR=\":\"outfit\"},0,{\"VAR=\":\"naps\"},0,{\"VAR=\":\"mischief\"},false,{\"VAR=\":\"has_glitter\"},false,{\"VAR=\":\"has_ribbon\"},false,{\"VAR=\":\"heard_riddle\"},false,{\"VAR=\":\"pip_hint\"},false,{\"VAR=\":\"ch1_done\"},false,{\"VAR=\":\"ch2_done\"},\"/ev\",\"end\",null]}],\"listDefs\":{}}";
